<BaseLayout title="Reading | Mahsa Ghasemi">
  <h1 class="page-heading">Reading</h1>
  <p class="lead">Papers and books currently shaping my research thinking.</p>

  <p>
    This page tracks papers, books, and essays that are shaping how I think. It is not meant to be
    complete.
  </p>

  <h2>How I read</h2>
  <p>
    I try to read papers for the idea that changes my framing, not only for the method. A paper
    becomes useful when I can answer: what assumption does this expose, and what question does it
    make sharper?
  </p>

  <h2>Current paper clusters</h2>

  <div class="section-list">
    <section>
      <h3>Weak supervision and whole-slide learning</h3>
      <ul class="notebook-list">
        <li>Attention-based multiple instance learning</li>
        <li>CLAM and weakly supervised computational pathology</li>
        <li>Clinical-scale weak supervision on whole-slide images</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>Self-supervised and foundation models</h3>
      <ul class="notebook-list">
        <li>DINO</li>
        <li>Masked autoencoders</li>
        <li>HIPT and hierarchical pathology representations</li>
        <li>Pathology foundation models and representation probing</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>Annotation and evaluation</h3>
      <ul class="notebook-list">
        <li>Sparse supervision for detection and segmentation</li>
        <li>Dataset documentation and data-centric AI</li>
        <li>Calibration, external validation, and clinically meaningful metrics</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2>Reading rule</h2>
  <p>
    For every paper I read carefully, I want to write one sentence that begins: "This paper changed
    how I think about..."
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
